Maintainer request 2026-10-15. Today the Hermes plugin (hermes/skilletor/) is installed by hand: clone, symlink into $HERMES_HOME/plugins/skilletor, enable in config.yaml (README 'The plugin under Hermes', spec §16.4/§16.5 'Distribution'). Goal: `hermes plugins install <something>` installs and (if Hermes allows) enables it. Research first in the Hermes source (~/dev/hermes-citiai, hermes_cli/plugins*.py, website docs): what 'plugins install' accepts (git URL, subdirectory, a repo-root layout, a registry/catalog entry, pip package?), where it puts the plugin, whether it can enable it, how updates work. Then: the plugin must still find a CLI — an installed copy is not a symlink into a checkout, so decide how it gets skilletor (PATH, SKILLETOR_BIN, a bundled dist/skilletor.js run with node, or fetching the matching release). Keep the plugin's version = package version (test exists). Verify with the real-Hermes test harness (hermes-real.test.ts): install from a local path/URL into a throwaway HERMES_HOME, then the session-start sync works. Update README, spec §16.4/§16.5, Changes. If a catalog/registry entry is needed outside this repo, say so — don't file it unasked.

2026-10-16 04:08 Research (Hermes ea81748579, hermes_cli/plugins_cmd*.py): 'plugins install' takes a catalog name, a git URL (https/http/ssh/git@/file://) or owner/repo shorthand; a subdirectory as owner/repo/<dir>, URL#<dir>, or a GitHub /tree/<ref>/<dir> link. A subdir install is a blobless sparse clone; only that dir is published to $HERMES_HOME/plugins/<manifest name> (no .git, no bin/ or dist/). --enable enables it (else a TTY prompt, non-TTY stays disabled). Security scan on install: the plugin as-is is 'safe'. 'hermes plugins update skilletor' re-clones the recorded source (subdir installs carry no .git) and keeps it enabled. Verified by hand: 'hermes plugins install file://<repo>#hermes/skilletor --enable' works with NO change to this repo's layout. Note: install prepares Hermes' isolated runtime (downloads python+uv into the home, ~450 MB, 40 s, network) and fetches the catalog kill list.

2026-10-16 04:09 CLI decision: an installed copy runs $SKILLETOR_BIN, else 'skilletor' on PATH (find_cli as before; the checkout branch only serves the symlink install). Hermes loads $HERMES_HOME/.env into the process env (hermes_cli/env_loader.py, called by main.py), so SKILLETOR_BIN=<clone>/bin/skilletor in that .env reaches the plugin — verified by hand: session start synced, 'hermes skilletor status' ran. Rejected: bundling dist/skilletor.js into hermes/skilletor/ — Hermes' install scan gives it a CAUTION verdict (5 HIGH 'exfiltration' regex hits on nunjucks' 'env = … new Environment()', 2 obfuscation hits), which blocks a non-TTY install and needs --force or a TTY 'Install anyway?'; reworking the bundle to dodge a scanner is not an option. Rejected: falling back to the Claude Code plugin cache CLI (installed_plugins.json) — it may be an older release without --harness hermes, several scopes/versions; fails silent. Rejected: fetching a release at run time (network, bypasses the install scan). Not used: requires_env: SKILLETOR_BIN in plugin.yaml would prompt at install but labels an optional var as required and re-prompts on --force reinstalls.

2026-10-16 04:12 Also found (drift, not in scope): bin/skilletor finds dist/ via dirname $0 without resolving a symlink, so 'ln -s <clone>/bin/skilletor ~/.local/bin/skilletor' fails (Cannot find module <linkdir>/../dist/skilletor.js; reproduced) – and an 'npm install -g'/'npm link' bin symlink would too. That is why README points the installed Hermes plugin at SKILLETOR_BIN in $HERMES_HOME/.env rather than a PATH symlink. Fixing the launcher (follow its own link) would make 'skilletor on PATH' a one-liner. Also: 'hermes plugins check-updates' reports a subdirectory install as 'provenance drift … dir has no .git' (Hermes' limitation; 'hermes plugins update' works).

